  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer NNN.\n\nLet us call a set of pairs of integers S={(A1,B1),(A2,B2),(A3,B3),…,(AK,BK)}S = \\{(A_1, B_1), (A_2, B_2), (A_3, B_3), \\ldots, (A_K, B_K)\\}S={(A1​,B1​),(A2​,B2​),(A3​,B3​),…,(AK​,BK​)} good if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n\n1≤Ai&lt;Bi≤N1 \\le A_i &lt; B_i \\le N1≤Ai​&lt;Bi​≤N\nFor every permutation PPP of the integers [1,N][1, N][1,N], it is possible to sort PPP with the usage of the following operation as many times as you want:\n\n- Choose (x,y)∈S(x, y) \\in S(x,y)∈S, and then swap PxP_xPx​ and PyP_yPy​.\nThe cost of a set is defined as ∑i=1K(Ai⊕Bi)\\sum_{i = 1}^{K} (A_i \\oplus B_i)∑i=1K​(Ai​⊕Bi​), where ⊕\\oplus⊕ represents the Bitwise XOR operator.\n\nFind the minimum cost of a good set.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum cost of a good set.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1031 \\le T \\le 10^31≤T≤103",
      "2≤N≤1092 \\le N \\le 10^92≤N≤109"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "5\n2\n3\n4\n5\n100",
        "output": "3\n3\n8\n8\n375",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : S={(1,2)}S = \\{(1, 2)\\}S={(1,2)} is a good set with cost 333 due to the following reasons:\n\nP=[1,2]P = [1, 2]P=[1,2] is already sorted and needs no operations.\nP=[2,1]P = [2, 1]P=[2,1] can be sorted by using (x,y)=(1,2)(x, y) = (1, 2)(x,y)=(1,2).\n\nTest Case 2 : S={(1,3),(2,3)}S = \\{(1, 3), (2, 3)\\}S={(1,3),(2,3)} is a good set with cost 333."
      }
    ]
  },
